{"hemiparesis":{
    "Does this patient have weakness on one side of their body? For instance, the note may mention hemiparesis, unilateral paresis, unilateral weakness, or asymmetric weakness. If the text mentions weakness but does not specify that it is unilateral, respond \"not present\".":"hemiparesis"
},
"nih_stroke":{
    "Is this patient showing a decreased level of consciousness? For instance, they may not be alert, may only arousable by repeated stimulation to attend, or be obtunded and require strong or painful stimulation to make movements. They may also respond only with reflex motor or autonomic effects, or be totally unresponsive, flaccid, or areflexic.":"1a level of consciousness",
    "Is the patient unable to correctly state their age and the current month if asked? For instance, they may answer one or both questions incorrectly, not respond, or be unable to respond.":"1b level of consciousness",
    "Is the patient unable to blink their eyes and squeeze their hand if asked? ":"1c level of consciousness",
    "Does this patient show abnormal eye movement? For instance, they may show partial or forced gaze palsy, a peripheral nerve paresis, or forced deviation":"2 best gaze",
    "Does this patient have hemianopia, quadrantanopia, or other vision loss due to stroke? Ignore vision loss due to age, macular degeneration, or eye damage.":"3 visual",
    "Is the patient unable to make facial movements such as showing their teeth, raising their eyebrows, or closing their eyes? For instance, the text may mention flattened nasolabial fold, asymmetry on smiling, absence of facial movement, or partial or complete facial paralysis or palsy.":"4 facial palsy",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their left arm raised for at least 10 seconds? For instance, their arm may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"5a left motor arm",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their right arm raised for at least 10 seconds? For instance, their arm may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"5a right motor arm",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their left leg raised for at least 5 seconds? For instance, their leg may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"6a left motor leg",
    "Is the patient unable to keep their right leg raised for at least 5 seconds? For instance, their leg may drift down slowly or fall completely.":"6a right motor leg",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver on the left side? Ignore any slowness.":"7a right finger-nose",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver on the right side? Ignore any slowness.":"7b left finger-nose",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver on their left side, such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"7c left heel–shin",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver on their right side, such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"7d right heel–shin",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with sensory perception? For instance, the patient may report that a pinprick is not sharp or not painful, or not be aware of being touched.":"8 sensation",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with understanding or making language or speech? For instance, they may struggle to describe a a picture, to name items, or read from a list of sentences, or they may show a loss of fluency, lack of comprehension, or limitation on ideas expressed. Ignore speech issues which are exclusively motor, such as dysarthria.":"9 language/aphasia",
    "Does this patient's speech show dysarthria, or slurring to the point that some words are not intelligible? For instance, the text may mention having to ask the patient to repeat themselves multiple times. Do not consider hypophonia.":"10 dysarthria",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with extinction and inattention? For instance, they may show visual, tactile, auditory, spatial, or personal inattention, extinction to bilateral simultaneous stimulation, hemi-inattention or extinction, or orient to only one side of space.":"11 extinction and inattention"
},
"depression":{
    "Does this patient show lack of pleasure from activities or interactions that used to be enjoyable? If the text does not mention enjoyment of activities, respond \"not present\"":"anhedonia",
    "Does this patient show negative attitudes or pessimism about the future and a sense of hopelessness? If the text does not mention pessimism or hopelessness, respond \"not present\"":"pessimism",
    "Is this patient uneasy or generally dissatisfied with life? If the text does not mention depressive symptoms, respond \"not present\"":"dissatisfaction",
    "Does this patient seem very sad or depressed? If the text does not mention overall mood, respond \"not present\"":"depressed",
    "Is this patient being treated for depression? For instance, the text may mention the patient being on antidepressant medication or receiving therapy for depression. If the text does not mention treatment, respond \"not present\"":"depression treatment"
},
"memory":{
    "Does this patient show signs of acute episodic anterograde amnesia? For instance, they may start having difficulty remembering events that happened after a stroke, or be unable to form new memories after the stroke. If the text mentions short-term memory problems only, respond \"not present\".":"anterograde_amnesia",
    "Does this patient show signs of acute episodic retrograde amnesia? For instance, they may have difficulty remembering events that occured before a stroke. If the text mentions short-term memory problems only, or memory problems not associated with a stroke, respond \"not present\".":"retrograde_amnesia"
},
"billing code":{
},
"bars":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty walking or disturbed gait? For instance, they may mention the patient staggering, difficulties in half turn, requiring support from a wall or stick, or being entirely unable to walk on their own.":"gait",
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing the heel-shin maneuver such as lowering their heel jerkily, or with lateral movements?":"heel–shin",
    "Does this patient's speech show dysarthria, or slurring to the point that some words are not intelligible? For instance, the text may mention having to ask the patient to repeat themselves multiple times. Do not consider hypophonia.":"speech",
    "Does this patient have oculomotor abnormalities? For example, the text may mention slowed pursuit, saccadic intrusions, hypo/hypermetric saccade, or nystagmus.":"oculomotor",
    "Does this patient show dysmetria, oscillating movement, termor, or segmented movement of the arm or hand when performing the finger-nose maneuver? Ignore any slowness.":"finger-nose"
    },
"bars_severity":{
    "For this patient, rate the severity of disturbed gait or walking difficulty (staggering, trouble turning, needs cane/wall, cannot walk unaided) on a 0–3 scale. 0=none/absent, 1=mild, 2=moderate, 3=severe. Return only a single integer between 0 and 3; if not mentioned, return 0.": "gait",
    "For this patient, rate the severity of difficulty performing the heel–shin maneuver (jerky lowering or lateral movements) on a 0–3 scale. 0=none/absent, 1=mild, 2=moderate, 3=severe. Return only a single integer 0–3; if not mentioned, return 0.": "heel-shin",
    "For this patient, rate the severity of dysarthria or slurred, partly unintelligible speech (not hypophonia) on a 0–3 scale. 0=none/absent, 1=mild, 2=moderate, 3=severe. Return only a single integer 0–3; if not mentioned, return 0.": "speech",
    "For this patient, rate the severity of oculomotor abnormalities (slowed pursuit, saccadic intrusions, hypo/hypermetric saccades, nystagmus) on a 0–3 scale. 0=none/absent, 1=mild, 2=moderate, 3=severe. Return only a single integer 0–3; if not mentioned, return 0.": "oculomotor",
    "For this patient, rate the severity of finger–nose test abnormalities (dysmetria, oscillating/segmented movement; ignore slowness) on a 0–3 scale. 0=none/absent, 1=mild, 2=moderate, 3=severe. Return only a single integer 0–3; if not mentioned, return 0.": "finger-nose"
    },
"moca":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty performing visuospatial or executive tasks such as a path task, copying a line drawing of a bed, or drawing a clock?":"binary",
    "Does this patient struggle with naming tasks such as naming animals based on pictures?":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with memory, such as being unable to remember a list of words read to them?":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty paying attention? For instance, they may be unable to repeat a list of numbers or letters read to them, either forwards or backwards, or may struggle with a serial subtraction task.":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with language skills? For instance, they may have difficulty repeating a sentence told to them. Only consider cognitive problems, and ignore dysarthria, slurring, and any other motor issues":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with abstraction? For instance, they may struggle to identify similarities between a hammer and screwdriver, or matches and lamps.":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with delayed recall? For instance, if given a list of words, they may have difficulty repeating them back five minutes later.":"binary",
    "Does this patient have difficulty orienting themselves? For instance, they may not know the date, month, year, day, city, or place they are in.":"binary"
    },
"ccas":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty with semantic fluency? For instance, they may not be able to name more than 15 animals in one minute.":"semantic fluency",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with phonemic fluency? For instance, they may not be able to name more than 9 words starting with a given letter out of 19.":"phonemic fluency",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with category switching? For instance, they may not be able to alternate between naming vegetables and types of jobs for more than 9 words in one minute.":"category switching",
    "Does this patient have difficulty with verbal registration? For instance, they may not be able to repeat a list of 5 words given to them within 4 attempts.":"verbal registration",
    "Does this patient have difficulty repeating strings of digits, forwards or backwards?":"digit span",
    "Is this patient unable to correctly draw a cube, with the correct number of lines?":"cube draw",
    "Is this patient unable to correctly copy a drawing of a cube?":"cube copy",
    "Is this patient unable to identify similarities between objects, such as nose/ear, sheep/elephant, lake/river, or airplane/motorcycle?":"similarities",
    "Does this patient have difficulty completing a go-no go task, where they raise their finger if the doctor taps the table once, but not if they tap twice?":"go-no go",
    "Does this patient have unusual affect? For instance, they may have difficulty with focusing attention or mental flexibility; be emotionally labile or show incongruous emotions; show easy sensory overload or avoidant behaviors; express illogical thoughts; lack empathy, be apathetic, or have blunted affect; or be angry or aggressive, irritable, oppositional, with difficulty with social cues and social boundaries. Ignore depression or anxiety symptoms.":"affect"
    },
"cnrs":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty maintaining mental focus or sustained attention, make careless mistakes, or not attend to details? Ignore issues with focusing the eyes":"attention a1",
    "Is this patient easily distracted by non-essential or irrelevant stimuli? If the text does not mention distraction, or the patient is distracted only by pain,respond \"not present\"":"attention a2",
    "Is this patient often \"on the go,\" acting as if \"driven by a motor\" (e.g., is unable to be or uncomfortable being still for extended time), restless, or difficult to keep up with? If the text does not mention restlessness or discomfort sitting still, respond \"not present\"":"attention a3",
    "Does this patient feel compelled to perform habits that are excessive and repetitive, and become distressed when prevented from doing so? If the text does not mention repetitive habits, respond \"not present\"":"attention a4",
    "Does this patient feel driven to complete tasks or behave in a rigid, inflexible way according to specific, personal rules? If the text does not mention rigid thinking or behavior, respond \"not present\"":"attention a5",
    "Does this patient worry repeatedly or too much about his/her own emotional/physical state, in a way that seems excessive or inappropriate? If the text does not mention worry, respond \"not present\". Only consider worries that are excessive or inappropriate, and ignore reasonable concerns about a patient's health":"attention b6",
    "Does this patient repeat the same response (e.g., word, phrase, gesture, behavior) despite being ineffective or inappropriate? If the text does not mention repetitive behavior, respond \"not present\"":"attention b7",
    "Does this patient get mentally “stuck” or have trouble shifting thoughts or mental focus from one activity or situation to another? If the text does not mention issues shifting focus, respond \"not present\"":"attention b8",
    "Is this patient unable to stop repetitive thoughts, urges or images that seem to cause distress? If the text does not mention repetitive thoughts or urges, respond \"not present\"":"attention b9",
    "Does this patient act hastily or carelessly in the moment without thinking (e.g., interrupting others, trouble waiting his/her turn). ":"emotion a10",
    "Does this patient show rapid changes in emotion that are unrelated to the situation?":"emotion a11",
    "Does this patient cry and/or laugh inappropriately, without an apparent trigger or cause?":"emotion a12",
    "Is this patient overly anxious, and/or do they experience sudden episodes of intense fear or discomfort (e.g., heart racing, shortness of breath, dizziness)?":"emotion a13",
    "Does this patient show lack of pleasure from activities or interactions that used to be enjoyable? If the text does not mention enjoyment of activities, respond \"not present\"":"emotion b14",
    "Does this patient show negative attitudes or pessimism about the future and a sense of hopelessness? If the text does not mention pessimism or hopelessness, respond \"not present\". If the text indicates that a drug or treatment is improving their negative attitudes, respond \"yes\"":"emotion b15",
    "Is this patient uneasy or generally dissatisfied with life? If the text does not mention depressive symptoms, respond \"not present\"":"emotion b16",
    "Does this patient seem very sad or depressed? If the text does not mention overall mood, respond \"not present\". If the text indicates that the patient is being treated for depression, respond \"yes\"":"emotion b17",
    "Does this patient make repetitive movements, postures, or utterances that follow a particular pattern (e.g., hand flapping, finger flicking, body rocking, repeating noises or phrases)? Ignore cases of involuntary tremor. If the text does not mention repetitive movements, respond \"not present\"":"autism a18",
    "Is this patient drawn to sensory experiences, such as excessive smelling or touching of objects, or fascination with lights or spinning objects? If the text does not mention sensory experiences, respond \"not present\"":"autism a19",
    "Does this patient avoid or show high sensitivity to light, touch, or texture (including clothing and the mouth-feel of foods)? If the text does not mention sensitivities, or only mentions food sensitivities in relation to trouble swallowing, respond \"not present\"":"autism b20",
    "Is this patient easily overwhelmed by, or has an extreme reaction to, sounds, textures, tastes, smells, temperature or lights? If the text does not mention sensitivity to stimulation, or only mentions food sensitivities in relation to trouble swallowing, respond \"not present\"":"autism b21",
    "Does this patient communicate in a way that is difficult to follow, illogical, rambling, or nonsensical? If the text does not mention communication, or if the issues are only related to speech issues such as dysarthria rather than psychological, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis a22",
    "Does this patient express irrational concerns or false beliefs that he/she is threatened in some way (e.g., others are out to hurt him/her)? If the text does not mention false beliefs or paranoia, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis a23",
    "Does this patient see or hear things that they believe are real but are not present? If the text does not mention hallucinations, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis a24",
    "Does this patient have trouble understanding other people's feelings or perspective? If the text does not mention the patient's social interactions, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis b25",
    "Does this patient appear distant or aloof, with a lack of emotional reactivity to situations or others that would typically evoke an emotional response? If the text does not mention the patient's emotional responses to situations, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis b26",
    "Is this patient disinterested, unconcerned, or indifferent to daily activities, future events, self, or others? If the text does not mention disinterest or indifference, respond \"not present\"":"psychosis b27",
    "Does this patient act or appear to be unreasonably angry or aggressive? If the text does not mention anger or aggression, respond \"not present\"":"social a28",
    "Is this patient easily upset or irritable? If the text does not mention irritability, respond \"not present\"":"social a29",
    "Is this patient intolerant of people entering their personal space or overly assertive or protective about communal space and objects? If the text does not mention social difficulties, respond \"not present\"":"social a30",
    "Is this patient deliberately disobedient, defiant, or argumentative? If the text does not mention argumentativeness or defiance, respond \"not present\"":"social a31",
    "Does this patient act in a passive, immature, or childlike manner If the text does not mention the patient's behavior, respond \"not present\"?":"social b32",
    "Does this patient seem unaware of social cues, or speak or behave in a way that is inappropriate to the situation? If the text does not mention social cues or appropriateness, respond \"not present\"":"social b33",
    "Does this patient seem unaware of others' personal space, have difficulty adapting to social situations, or act or make statements in an inappropriate manner? If the text does not mention social difficulties or inappropriate behavior, respond \"not present\"":"social b34",
    "Is this patient overly trusting of others, vulnerable to being tricked or manipulated, or prone to believe highly unlikely statements or claims? If the text does not mention trustfulness or gullibility, respond \"not present\"":"social b35"
    },
"cnrs_combined_columns":{
    "Does this patient have difficulty maintaining focus or sustained attention? For example, they may make careless mistakes or not attend to details; be easily distracted by non-essential or irrelevant stimuli; often be “on the go,” acting as if “driven by a motor” (e.g., is unable to be or uncomfortable being still for extended time); feel compelled to perform habits that are excessive and repetitive, and become distressed when prevented from doing so; or feel driven to complete tasks or behave in a rigid, inflexible way according to specific, personal rules.":"binary",
    "Does this patient get mentally “stuck” or have trouble shifting thoughts or mental focus from one activity or situation to another? For example, they may repeat the same responses (e.g., word, phrase, gesture, behavior) despite being ineffective or inappropriate, worry repeatedly or too much about their own emotional/physical state, or be unable to stop repetitive thoughts, urges or images that seem to cause distress.":"binary",
    "Does this patient struggle to control their emotions? For instance, they may act hastily or carelessly in the moment without thinking (e.g., interrupting others, trouble waiting his/her turn); show rapid changes in emotion that are more intense than usual or unrelated to the situation; cry and/or laugh without an apparent trigger inappropriately; be overly anxious; or experiences sudden episodes of intense fear or discomfort (e.g., heart racing, shortness of breath, dizziness). Ignore depression or anxiety symptoms.":"binary",
    "Does this patient seem very sad or depressed in life? For instance, they may lack pleasure from activities or interactions that used to be enjoyable, hold Negative attitudes or pessimism about the future and a sense of hopelessness, or be uneasy or generally dissatisfied with life.":"binary",
    "Does this patient have habits characteristic of the autism spectrum? For instance, they may show repetitive movements, postures, or utterances that follow a particular pattern (e.g., hand flapping, finger flicking, body rocking, repeating noises or phrases) or be drawn to sensory experiences, such as excessive smelling or touching of objects, or fascination with lights or spinning objects.":"binary",
    "Does this patient show sensitivities characteristic of the autism spectrum? For instance, they may avoid or be very sensitive to light touch or texture (including clothing and the mouth-feel of foods), or they may be easily overwhelmed by, or have an extreme reaction to, sounds, textures, tastes, smells, temperature or lights.":"binary",
    "Does this patient have characteristics of being on the psychosis spectrum? For example, they may communicate in a way that is difficult to follow, illogical, rambling, or nonsensical; express irrational concerns or false beliefs that he/she is threatened in some way (e.g., others are out to hurt him/her); or see or hear things that they believe are real but are not present.":"binary",
    "Does this patient seem disinterested, unconcerned, or indifferent to daily activities, future events, themselves or others? For example, they may appear distant or aloof with a lack of emotional reactivity to situations or others that would typically evoke an emotional response, or have trouble understanding another person's feelings or perspective.":"binary",
    "Does this patient act or appear to be unreasonably angry or aggressive? For instance, they may be easily upset or irritable; be intolerant of people entering their personal space or overly assertive or protective about communal space and objects; or be deliberately disobedient, defiant and argumentative.":"binary",
    "Does this patient seem to lack social skills? For example, they may act in a passive, immature, or childlike manner; seem unaware of social cues, or speak or behave in a way that is inappropriate to the situation; be unaware of others' personal space, have difficulty adapting to social situations, act or makes statements in an inappropriate manner; or be overly trusting of others, vulnerable to being tricked or manipulated, or prone to believe highly unlikely statements or claims.":"binary"
    },
"hbs_seizure":{
    "Has this patient had a seizure? For example, the text may mention epilepsy, convulsions, or fits. If none of these are mentioned in the text, respond \"not present\" instead of \"no\".":"seizure",
    "Did this patient have a seizure before their first stroke? The text may mention a history of epilepsy, seizures, convulsions, or fits. If none of these are mentioned in the text, or if the timing of the seizure is not mentioned, respond \"not present\" instead of \"no\".":"pre-stroke seizure",
    "Did this patient start having seizures after first stroke? The text may mention acute, late-onset, or post-stroke epilepsy or seizures. If none of these are mentioned in the text, or if the timing of the seizure is not mentioned, respond \"not present\" instead of \"no\".":"post-stroke seizure",
    "Did this patient have a seizure or convulsion within one week after their stroke? For example, the text may mention acute or early-onset seizure or epilepsy. Do not consider delayed-onset seizure or epilepsy. If the timing of the seizure is not mentioned in the text, respond \"not present\" instead of \"no\".":"acute seizure"
}
}